  "runner_up_case": "The strongest honest case for FALSE is that the claim is materially misleading as a statement about Benko's sentence as of today (2026-10-01): the October 2025 two-year sentence was a non-final first-instance judgment on only one of two counts (he was acquitted on the other), and it was superseded when a retrial ended on 2026-09-22 with a two-and-a-half-year (30-month) sentence. Anyone relying on this claim for Benko's current custodial exposure would be materially wrong — the operative sentence is 30 months, not two years. The evidentiary base is also entirely secondary journalism: no court order or Austrian primary-adjacent source (ORF, APA, Der Standard) has been examined; thelocal.at (2025-10-15) attributes the sentence only to 'an Austrian court,' so the specific Innsbruck Regional Court attribution rests on costar.com and retrospective 2026 pieces; and none of the snippets states whether the two years was imposed in full or partially suspended, which Austrian courts commonly do. That said, honesty requires conceding this is a weak runner-up: the claim is explicitly time-anchored ('in October 2025'), and as a historical assertion it is corroborated by two independent contemporaneous reports dated 15-17 October 2025 plus three retrospective 2026 reports, so the realistic alternatives are TRUE or at most MIXED (if read as stating the current sentence), not FALSE.",
  "would_flip_it": [
    "A primary or authoritative record of the October 2025 Innsbruck judgment (court order, APA/ORF/Der Standard report, or official court statement) showing the disposition was not a two-year prison sentence — e.g., acquittal, a different sentence length, a materially different suspended/unconditional split, or a verdict actually delivered in a different month or by a different court.",
    "Credible reporting that 'two years' was the prosecutor's sentencing demand rather than the court's imposed sentence, with the court's actual disposition differing.",
